1. Startup probe — “Has this slow-starting application finished starting?” 
It gives an application time to initialize. Until the startup probe succeeds, Kubernetes does not run its configured liveness and readiness probes.

2. Readiness probe — “Can this Pod receive traffic?”
If readiness fails, Kubernetes marks the Pod as not ready and normally removes it from the ready endpoints of its Service. The container is not restarted just because readiness fails.

3. Liveness probe — “Should this container be restarted?”
If the liveness check repeatedly fails, Kubernetes restarts the affected container. It is useful when an application becomes stuck and cannot recover by itself.









